60,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Candidate B bills on-site, generating revenue. A and D are off-site, earning nothing. With our cut allocation, B is the only financially viable option.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, revenue is a symptom, not a cure. Candidate A mirrors the call-back trial that actually reduced readmissions. I need to know if B has a mechanism to sustain patient engagement beyond the initial funding.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I agree on sustainability. But Candidate A relies on the nine vacant nursing posts. We cannot add home visits to an already broken roster.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, exactly. Candidate A means more traffic. The car park is already full. I need a program that stays inside the existing rooms and flow.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, you are right. Candidate C is shared between midwifery and pediatrics. That is exactly how the joint clinic failed. I cannot support a program without a single, clear owner.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shion, I hear you, but the ledger is clear. Off-site visits earn zero. We cannot fund a program that generates no revenue when our allocation is already cut.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, the call-back trial proved off-site care works. But I agree with Midori; the nursing vacancies make A impossible. I am looking at D for its single ownership.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I agree. Candidate C uses the midwifery slack I found. It keeps the roster stable and avoids the agency costs straining our wards.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, I worry about C. It adds new patients to a car park that is already full. The physical flow cannot absorb that extra traffic.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Kenji, the traffic concern is valid. But Candidate D relies on outside data agreements, which took fourteen months to sign. That delay is a quiet risk I cannot ignore.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, the data delay is a real risk. But Candidate B stays on-site, billing standard rates. It avoids the off-site zero-revenue trap and the staffing gaps.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, B is a physical expansion. Kenji says the rooms are full. I am looking at D. It consolidates care without adding traffic or new rooms.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I understand the appeal of D. But C uses the midwifery slack I found. It keeps the roster stable and avoids the agency costs straining our wards.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Shion, D is a desk job. No new rooms, no extra cars. It fits the existing flow perfectly. B would just clog the already full parking.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I hear your point about D. But the joint clinic failed because ownership was shared. C is also shared. I need a single, clear departmental home to prevent that quiet collapse.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I vote for B. It is the only option that generates on-site revenue to offset our reduced allocation. The others simply drain resources we no longer have.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Shion,,"I vote for D. It has a single departmental home, avoiding the shared-ownership trap that killed the joint clinic. It also fits the physical constraints Kenji identified.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Midori,,"I vote for C. It uses the midwifery slack I found, keeping our roster stable and avoiding the agency costs straining our wards.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I vote for D. It requires no new rooms or parking. The booking screens can handle it without seizing up. It fits the existing physical flow perfectly.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"I vote for D. It has a single, clear departmental home. The joint clinic failed because ownership was shared. I need one team to truly own the rota and the budget.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
